  "prompt": "How do the stages of life shape our understanding of time and mortality?",
  "answer": "As children, time often feels expansive: days are long, the future is open, and death can seem distant or abstract. With age, routines and memories accumulate, and the same span of time can feel shorter because it forms a smaller share of life and contains fewer unfamiliar experiences.\n\nLife stages also change what mortality means. In youth, it may be an idea to resist or ignore; in adulthood, it can become concrete through loss, responsibility, and awareness of limits. Later in life, some people focus more on meaning, relationships, and what they will leave behind, while others feel grief or urgency. These patterns aren’t universal: health, culture, circumstance, and personality shape them too.\n\nOur sense of time and death is therefore not just about years passing. It changes with what we have lived, what we still hope to do, and how we understand our place among others.",
